# Raw secret/vulnerability scan output. NEVER commit — a gitleaks report embeds the MATCHED
# SECRET VALUE inline for every finding, so committing one publishes exactly what the scan
# was run to find, and pushing it means those credentials must be treated as compromised
# regardless of any later deletion.
#
# Prompted by gitleaks-report.json + gitleaks-report-redacted.json appearing untracked in the
# working tree. Verified at the time this was added: no scan report has EVER been committed on
# any branch (`git log --all --diff-filter=A`), so this is preventive, not remedial — there is
# no history to purge.
#
# gitleaks is run AD HOC (it is not in CI or package.json), which is precisely why its output
# lands loose in the repo root where a stray `git add -A` would take it. The other tools below
# are not wired here either; they are listed because a secret/dependency sweep is the kind of
# thing that gets run by hand next to gitleaks, and the cost of covering them is nil.
#
# Deliberately NOT a blanket `*-report.json`: that would silently swallow a legitimate future
# artifact. Name the tools; add one when a new tool is actually used.
gitleaks-report*.json
gitleaks-report*.sarif
semgrep-report*.json
semgrep.sarif
trivy-report*.json
snyk-report*.json
npm-audit*.json

# taste-skill (npx skills add) - agent skill files, not app code
.agents/
skills-lock.json

# Competitor visual reference captures — INTENTIONALLY LOCAL, never committed.
#
# research/competitors/ holds full-page screenshots of competitors' marketing sites
# (SourceLoop, Orchly, …) used as visual reference under design.md §35.1, plus an
# INDEX.md manifest. It is ignored rather than committed for two independent reasons:
#
#   1. THIS REPO IS PUBLIC (verified: visibility=public). Committing third-party
#      marketing pages would republish someone else's copyrighted material from our
#      own public repo. §35.3 treats competitor assets as a trademark/misrepresentation
#      exposure and permits studying them, not shipping them.
#   2. It is ~24 MB of binaries. Git history is append-only, so committing it costs
#      every future clone forever and cannot be undone without rewriting history.
#
# ⚠️ Ignoring protects against `git clean -fd`, NOT `git clean -xfd` (-x removes ignored
# files too). If these captures need to survive indefinitely, move them OUTSIDE the repo
# working tree — a path under the repo is always one -x away from gone.
research/

# Design handoff bundle — INTENTIONALLY LOCAL, never committed.
#
# design_handoff_trackiq/ is the v4 design reference: a README spec plus 11 prototype
# HTML pages and their JSX/CSS. It is ignored, not committed, for two reasons:
#
#   1. Its own CLAUDE.md says these are "prototypes that show intended look and
#      behaviour … not production code". Committing them puts a second, competing
#      copy of every page in the tree — the exact confusion the handoff asks us to
#      avoid by recreating rather than porting.
#   2. Eleven of its pages carry the Babel-in-browser CDN tag, which is fingerprint
#      #1 in api/tests/v3-lift-detection.test.js. That guard scans our source tree;
#      committing the handoff would either trip it or force widening its exclusions,
#      and a guard with a hole in it is the false-pass class this repo has hit before.
#
# ⚠️ Same caveat as research/ above: ignoring survives `git clean -fd`, NOT `-xfd`.
# The canonical copy is the zip the founder supplied, outside the working tree.
design_handoff_trackiq/
